messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nTo determine whether the statement "Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given information, we need to analyze the logical relationships between the statements provided.\n\n1.**If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.**\n   - This implies that Mary working in student jobs on campus means she needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n2.**If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.**\n   - This suggests that Mary ordering takeout frequently in college indicates she works in student jobs on campus.\n\n3.**People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.**\n   - This creates a disjunction where Mary either orders takeout frequently or enjoys the dining hall meals and recipes.\n\n4.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.**\n   - This establishes a conditional relationship where enjoying the dining hall meals and recipes implies not being picky.\n\n5.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**\n   - This further specifies that enjoying the dining hall meals and recipes leads to spending time in the dining halls.\n\n6.**If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**\n   - This statement seems contradictory because it claims that Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, which contradicts the initial condition that she needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\nGiven these illogical relationships, let\'s ignore the statement:\n\n- The statement "Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls" should not be analyzed into two parts:\n  1.Mary is a picky eater.\n  2.If Mary is not a picky eater, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that if Mary does not work in student jobs on campus and doesn\'t need to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then she is both picky and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition. However, this directly supports the initial condition that she needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\nTherefore, the statement is **certain** because the initial condition that Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition aligns with the conclusion that she is both picky and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n</think>\n<answer>\nCertain\n</answer>\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 56%|#####6    | 115/204 [02:20<00:13,  6.82it/s]
